+# BLOCKED: release checksum cannot be made to pass from this repository alone
+
+## Status
+
+`python3 verify.py` still exits 1 because `artifacts/libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz`
+does not match the digest recorded in `SHA256SUMS`:
+
+```
+manifest: 7BEE020AC21EFB3831D920AF0B8C5EFE8D44CDF227AB94847F509188420356BD  libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz
+actual:   fde9b2575edbefb297fa7d93738b4bb1f09b7f777d1723b90194ad53cd9bc9ce  artifacts/libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz
+```
+
+`cli-2.0.1.zip` and `docs-2026.10.tar.gz` match their recorded digests.
+
+## What was fixed
+
+`verify.py` had two real bugs that prevented any artifact from being checked:
+
+1. Artifacts were opened in text mode, so binary files raised
+   `UnicodeDecodeError`. They are now opened with `"rb"`.
+2. The manifest stores uppercase hex digests while `hashlib` returns
+   lowercase. Comparison is now case-insensitive.
+
+After those fixes, `test_binary_files` and `test_reports_missing` pass. The
+only remaining failure is `test_release_artifacts_verify`, caused solely by
+the `libfoo` mismatch above.
+
+## Why this is blocked
+
+There is no authoritative source in the repository for what
+`libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz` should contain. I did not:
+
+- rewrite `SHA256SUMS` from the on-disk artifact: that makes the check
+  self-fulfilling and would publish whatever bytes are present, including
+  corrupted ones;
+- alter the artifact to match the manifest: the repository has no build
+  output, source, or upstream copy to establish the intended release bytes.
+
+Diagnostic note: the recorded digest corresponds to a single-byte variant of
+the current file (offset 1777, `0x62` -> `0x22`), which suggests the artifact
+was corrupted after the manifest was generated. I did not apply that change,
+because reconstructing a release artifact from a checksum without an
+authoritative build is not a safe release action.
+
+## Required to unblock
+
+Recover `libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz` from the authoritative build/release system
+(or confirm against that system that the manifest entry is stale and correct
+the manifest from the trusted source), then rerun:
+
+```
+python3 verify.py
+python3 -m unittest test_verify -v
+```
+
+No changes were made to `SHA256SUMS` or anything under `artifacts/`.
